                "text": "**Analyzing Shape Decorations**\n\nI'm currently focusing on deciphering the decoration patterns within the identical yellow shapes. It appears one shape is uniquely adorned with all decoration types (1, 2, and 3), unlike the others. I'm cross-referencing this observation with Example 1 to pinpoint any specific rules or exceptions.\n\n**Observing Anchor Decorations**\n\nMy recent focus has been on the \"2\" decorations. I've noted that each shape consistently has a \"2\" already attached, often at an \"end\" of the shape. This anchor seems to be a crucial point for subsequent decoration placement, and I'm trying to understand its precise positioning relative to the shape's structure.\n\n**Confirming Orientation Logic**\n\nI've confirmed that the \"2\" decoration dictates the shape's orientation. By tracing the \"2\" and observing its position relative to the shape's boundaries, I can infer how the shape is rotated. This insight is now being applied to the test inputs.\n\n**Mapping Shape Variants**\n\nI'm mapping the \"4\" cell arrangements and the \"2\" decoration's location across the different shapes. My current focus is on determining the specific geometric transformation\u2014likely a reflection or rotation\u2014that relates Shape 1's \"4\"s and \"2\" to those of Shape 2, aiming to formalize this relationship.\n\n**Rotating Shape Configurations**\n\nI've been thoroughly analyzing the coordinate transformations between Shape 1 and Shape 2, focusing on how their internal \"4\" cell positions and the \"2\" anchor relate. My current hypothesis, after performing a 180-degree rotation on Shape 1's relative coordinates, suggests this geometric operation accurately maps its configuration to that of Shape 2.\n\n**Confirming Shape Relationship**\n\nI've discovered that Shape 2 is a horizontal reflection of Shape 1's core structure. I'm now meticulously verifying this isometry by mapping each relative coordinate of Shape 1, including the anchor \"2